em in the product for  example you can encapsulate particular  ingredients and that changes how well  they work for example you can have a  lower percentage of encapsulated  regional and that can work just as well  as a higher percentage of unencapsulated  retinol so this ingredient list doesn't  have any transparency with that because  they are just using the standard  ingredient names you would find on any  other ingredient list the other issue is  that this gives you a whole bunch of  really useless info that no one really  needs i think this actually makes it  less transparent because now it's a lot  more confusing you have a whole bunch of  distractions and you can't just zero  into the stuff you actually want to know  you do get the percentages of every  active ingredient which i think is  useful even though there are differences  in potency and quality and that sort of  thing i still like to know roughly what  i'm expecting but now we also have all  the percentages of the inactive  ingredients which is like who cares this  tells me there's 83 water but if i had a  different product that had 87 water  that's not going to help me make a  decision i have propane dial at two  percent i would probably still buy it if  it's at five percent carbama is at 0.06  carbohydrate changes the texture of the  product but you could also work this out  by just putting it on your hand or  reading a review it would be way more  useful to read a review and find out how  tacky it is whether it feels drying or  sticky or thick or if it's difficult to  apply or even just watching a video of  someone applying it on their face or in  the back of their hand as a cosmetic  chemist if i wanted to copy this formula  it would give me an easier starting  point but it still isn't that useful  because there are so many different  ingredients that are listed under the  one ingredient name so carb member for  example this has 0.06 carbama i don't  know which one it is out of the hundreds  of di