y plant extracts and it  would be really nice and transparent if  they could tell us about them there are  also some calculation errors which i  think is a little bit petty of me to  point out but if they were going to show  everything then maybe they should have  had it right their overnight exfoliating  treatment says it has 10 alpha and beta  hydroxy acids and it lists them but if  you add them together you don't get 10  there's also another 0.1 fairy dust  issue here point my percent salicylic  acid is not enough to make a claim for  acne and sun damage plus acne is a drug  claim and this is not a drug this one is  probably super petty and i'm not sure if  anyone else has discovered it because  they probably weren't just nitpicking as  hard as me but the percentages on the  bha clarify serum don't add up to 100  they add up to 100.4 and there are 16  ingredients on here at 0.4 percent or  under so this seems like a pretty big  error also some products don't have  their percentages listed so saying that  they have nothing to hide by showing  this sorta says they have something to  hide with the other products there are  so many other things they could do to be  more transparent they could show us the  clinical results or maybe they could say  that they don't have them because they  didn't want to pay for them because it's  a lot cheaper to just print out the  ingredient list for marketing in one of  their instagram posts they also say it  also makes it easy to see which  ingredients we left out this includes  polysorbate 20 fragrance drying alcohol  and a long list of others it would be a  lot more transparent to not have clean  beauty fear-mongering maybe they could  tell people that parabens and sulfates  are fine also their niacinamide serum  has polysorbate 60 which is not that  different from polysorbate 20. other  people have also mentioned that their  sponsored posts especially those from  dermatologists have hidden disclosures  so  maybe that might be something to fix if  they're going to be more transparent  they also have a lot of drug claims on  their products so for example they say  rosehip oil will regenerate and heal  skin which is a very strong claim and it  also goes beyond the evidence we have  for what rosehip oil does to skin they  also say we hope this new level of  transparency invites feedback when we  can improve why not hire someone maybe a  cosmetic chemist to check their claims  instead of waiting for free feedback  which i do realize i have just given  them maybe i should be writing them an  invoice if they pay me i will let you  know in the interest of transparency i  rate this marketing strategy a hot mess  they do have stuff to hide and i don't  think they realize that they have stuff  to hide and now they've just made it  really obvious to everyone who knows  what to look for i believe this is  called showing your ass so let me know  what you think and let me know what  other dodgy marketing claims i should  look at next if you like this video like  and subscribe and click the notification  bell you can follow me on instagram and  tick tock and check out my blog good  molecules let me know who to send the  invoice to

